Java 8 introduced Optional class in the java.util package to provide a better way to handle situations where a value might be absent
helping to avoid NullPointerException and making the code more expressive
Optional is a container object that may or may not contain a non-null value. It's a way to explicitly express that a method
might not return a value, forcing the caller to handle both cases.

A. Key benefits of Optional
1. Explicitly indicates that a value might be absent
2. Reduces null check in code
3. Provides a fluent API for handling absent values
4. Makes the intent of your code clearer

B. Creating Optional Objects
1. Empty Optional
Optional<String> emptyOptional=Optional.empty();

2. Optional with non-null value
Optional<String> nonEmptyOptional=Optional.of("something");
//throws NullPointerException if value is null

3. Optional that may be null
String possibleNull=null;
Optional<String> nullableOptional=Optional.ofNullable(possibleNull);
//Accepts null values without throwing exception

C. Checking Value Presence

1. isPresent()

Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("Java");
if (opt.isPresent()) {
    System.out.println("Value: " + opt.get());
}

2. isEmpty()

Optional<String> opt = Optional.empty();
if (opt.isEmpty()) {
    System.out.println("No value present");
}

D. Retrieving Values

1. get() Not Recommended (can throw NoSuchElementException)

Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("Hello");
String value = opt.get(); // "Hello"

2. orElse() Provide default value
The fallback value is computed or evaluated every time, even if the Optional is not empty

Optional<String> opt = Optional.empty();
String value = opt.orElse("Default"); // "Default", if value present then gives that value

Optional<String> name = Optional.ofNullable(null);  // Empty Optional
String result = name.orElse("Default Name");
System.out.println(result);  // Output: Default Name

Optional<String> anotherName = Optional.of("John Doe");  // Non-empty Optional
String anotherResult = anotherName.orElse("Default Name");
System.out.println(anotherResult);  // Output: John Doe

3. orElseGet() Lazy default with Supplier
it evaluates the fallback value lazily-only when the Optional is empty, while orElse always evaluates the fallback value
even when it is not needed.

Optional<String> opt = Optional.empty();
String value = opt.orElseGet(() -> expensiveOperation()); // Only called if needed
Optional<String> name = Optional.ofNullable(null);

// Using orElse (returns default directly)
String resultWithOrElse = name.orElse("Default Name");
System.out.println(resultWithOrElse);  // Output: Default Name
// Using orElseGet (computes default lazily)
String resultWithOrElseGet = name.orElseGet(() -> "Lazy Default Name");
System.out.println(resultWithOrElseGet);  // Output: Lazy Default Name (computed lazily)

4. orElseThrow Throw exception if empty
You want to ensure that a value is present in the Optional and explicitly handle the case where it is missing by throwing an exception
It provides a clear way to fail fast in situations where the absence of a value is considered as error and must be handled immediately
You need to throw custom exceptions for more meaningful error messages or specific application
String result = value.orElseThrow(() -> new CustomException("Value not found"));

Optional<String> opt = Optional.empty();
String value = opt.orElseThrow(() -> new RuntimeException("Value not present"));

E. Conditional Actions

1. ifPresent() Execute action if value exists
Typically used when you want to perform an action only when a value is present (sending notification, making a database update)

Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("Hello");
opt.ifPresent(val -> System.out.println("Value: " + val));// prints to console

Optional<String> emptyOptional=Optional.empty();
emptyOptional.ifPresent(value->System.out.println("this is the value "+value));//does nothing as value is absent

2. ifPresentOrElse Execute action for both cases
Used when you need to perform one action if the value is present and a different action when it is absent
eg if user has credit card then make payment, else notify user to add credit card

Optional<String> opt = Optional.empty();
opt.ifPresentOrElse(
    val -> System.out.println("Value: " + val),
    () -> System.out.println("No value")
);

F. Transforming Values

1. map() Transform value if present

Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("hello");
Optional<String> upper = opt.map(String::toUpperCase); // Optional["HELLO"]

The map method allows you to apply a transformation to the value contained in the Optional if it is present and
if it is absent it simply returns an empty Optional.

Optional<String> name = Optional.of("John");

// Use map to transform the value inside the Optional, if present
Optional<String> uppercasedName = name.map(String::toUpperCase);
uppercasedName.ifPresent(System.out::println);  // Prints: JOHN

// Example with an empty Optional
Optional<String> emptyName = Optional.empty();
Optional<String> result = emptyName.map(String::toUpperCase);
// If present, print the value
result.ifPresent(System.out::println);  // Nothing is printed because the Optional is empty


Optional<User> user = Optional.of(new User("Alice", "ALICE@EXAMPLE.COM"));

Optional<String> emailLowerCase = user
    .map(User::getEmail)         // Extract the email from the User object
    .map(String::toLowerCase);   // Convert the email to lowercase

emailLowerCase.ifPresent(System.out::println);  // Prints: alice@example.com

We first extract the email using mao(User::getEmail)
Then we convert it to lowercase with another map(String::toLowerCase)
If user is Optional.empty(), then whole operation chain returns an empty Optional, and nothing happens

When to use map with Optional
When you wan to transform the value inside an Optional, such as converting the value to another type or applying a function to it
When you want to avoid performing transformations or accessing the value if the Optional is empty

2. flatMap() for nested Optional
Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("hello");
Optional<String> upper = opt.flatMap(s -> Optional.of(s.toUpperCase()));

map(): Results in Optional<Optional<String>> when you apply a function that returns an Optional.
You have to unwrap the result twice. Applies a function that returns a value. can result nested Optional
Requires unwrapping nested Optional

flatMap(): Avoids in Optional objects and directly a single Optional<String>, making it straightforward when the transformation
functions returns an Optional. Applies a function that returns an Optional. Flattens the result into a single Optional
Avoids nesting and unwrapping

3. filter() Conditionally keep value

Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("hello");
Optional<String> filtered = opt.filter(s -> s.length() > 3); // Optional["hello"]
Optional<String> empty = opt.filter(s -> s.length() > 10); // Optional.empty

Returns the same Optional if the condition is true or an empty Optional if false
Use filter when you need to conditionally remove the value inside the Optional based on a predicate
keep the value only if it meets the condition
It is especially useful when you want to avoid transforming the value but just want to decide
whether to keep or discard it based on some logic



Best Practices
1. Do not use Optional for fields-it is not serializable
2. Avoid optional in method parameters- can make API confusing
3. Do not use Optional.get() without checking-defeats the purpose
4. Prefer orElse/orElseGet over get()-safer alternatives
5. Use Optional as return type- Clearly indicates potential absence

Safe navigation
public String getStreetName(Address address) {
    return Optional.ofNullable(address)
            .map(Address::getStreet)
            .map(Street::getName)
            .orElse("Unknown street");
}

Method that may not return a value
public Optional<Employee> findEmployeeById(int id) {
    // Database lookup that might return null
    return Optional.ofNullable(employeeRepository.findById(id));
}

Combining Optionals
Optional<String> first = Optional.of("Hello");
Optional<String> second = Optional.of("World");

Optional<String> combined = first.flatMap(f -> second.map(s -> f + " " + s));
// Optional["Hello World"]

How Optional prevents NPE
public String getEmployeeName(Employee emp) {
    return emp.getName(); // Throws NPE if emp is null
}

With Optional
public Optional<String> getEmployeeName(Employee emp) {
    return Optional.ofNullable(emp)
                  .map(Employee::getName);
}
// Usage: Returns Optional.empty() if emp is null

public String getStreetName(Company company) {
    return company.getOffice().getAddress().getStreet();
    // NPE if any intermediate object is null
}

With Optional
public Optional<String> getStreetName(Company company) {
    return Optional.ofNullable(company)
                  .map(Company::getOffice)
                  .map(Office::getAddress)
                  .map(Address::getStreet);
}
// Returns Optional.empty() if any object in chain is null
Without Optional
public String findUserName(int id) {
    User user = userRepository.findById(id);
    return user != null ? user.getName() : "Unknown";
}
with Optional
public String findUserName(int id) {
    return userRepository.findById(id)
                        .map(User::getName)
                        .orElse("Unknown");
}

NPE risk
public class OrderProcessor {
    public String processOrder(Order order) {
        return order.getCustomer().getAddress().getCity().toUpperCase();
        // Any of these could be null:
        // - order
        // - getCustomer()
        // - getAddress()
        // - getCity()
    }
}

Safe code with Optional
public class OrderProcessor {
    public String processOrder(Order order) {
        return Optional.ofNullable(order)
            .map(Order::getCustomer)
            .map(Customer::getAddress)
            .map(Address::getCity)
            .map(String::toUpperCase)
            .orElse("UNKNOWN_CITY");
    }
}

